'''Linwood M. Boomer''' (born October 9, 1955) is a Canadian and American film and television producer, writer, and actor. He is known for playing the role of Adam Kendall on the television drama ''[[Little House on the Prairie (1974 TV series)|Little House on the Prairie]]'' (1978–1981), and for creating the [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox]] sitcom ''[[Malcolm in the Middle]]'' (2000–2006), as well as its revival series, ''[[Malcolm in the Middle: Life's Still Unfair|Life's Still Unfair]]'' (2026).

== Early life ==
Boomer was born into a lower-middle-class family in [[Vancouver, British Columbia]], Canada, the third of four sons. He grew up in [[San Mateo, California]], and attended Highlands Elementary School and [[Junípero Serra High School (San Mateo, California)|Junípero Serra High School]] before moving to [[Aragon High School]], from which he graduated in 1974.<ref name="Young23Apr2006">{{cite news |last1=Young |first1=Susan |title=Highlander isn't slumming in San Mateo |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/oakland-tribune-highlander-isnt-slummin/205520981/ |access-date=September 7, 2026 |work=Oakland Tribune |date=April 23, 2006 |pages=[https://www.newspapers.com/article/oakland-tribune-highlander-isnt-slummin/205520981/ 41], [https://www.newspapers.com/article/oakland-tribune-malcolm-in-the-middle/205521146/ 45]}}</ref> He was enrolled in a gifted program at school. His mother is named Eileen. Boomer created ''[[Malcolm in the Middle]]'', based on his own life story, which aired on [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox]] from January 2000 to May 2006.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.polygon.com/malcolm-in-the-middle-creator-linwood-boomer-interview/|title=26 years later, the creator of Malcolm in the Middle looks back: 'Frankie was almost supernaturally gifted'|first=Brian|last=VanHooker|date=April 14, 2026|website=Polygon.com}}</ref><ref>https://people.com/linwood-boomer-says-michael-landon-shaped-how-he-ran-malcolm-in-the-middle-12000586</ref>

==Career==
Boomer began his career as an actor. After playing Adam Kendall on ''Little House on the Prairie'',<ref>{{cite news |title=Linwood Boomer worries about future |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/gloucester-county-times-linwood-boomer-w/205520506/ |access-date=September 7, 2026 |work=The Gloucester County Times |date=December 20, 1978 |page=D-2 |via=Newspapers.com}} {{free access}}</ref> Boomer began working behind the camera in the television business.

Boomer's writing and executive producing credits include ''[[Night Court]]'', ''[[Flying Blind (TV series)|Flying Blind]]'', ''[[The Boys Are Back (TV series)|The Boys Are Back]]'', ''[[Townies]]'', ''[[3rd Rock from the Sun]]'' and ''[[God, the Devil and Bob]]''. He also created and executive produced the pilots ''Family Business'', ''Nice Try'', and the American version of ''[[Red Dwarf#American version|Red Dwarf]]'' and served as an executive producer under [[James L. Brooks]] for the pilot of ''[[Big (film)|Big]]''. He owns his own production company Satin City.

Boomer negotiated with [[CBS]] on a pilot order for a project that reunited him with [[Gail Berman]], the exec who shepherded ''Malcolm'' during her tenure at [[Regency Television|Regency TV]] and as programming chief at Fox. Boomer wrote the script and exec produced along with Berman and [[Lloyd Braun]] for their ''BermanBraun'' shingle and [[Universal Media Studios]], where BermanBraun is based. Dubbed ''The Karenskys'', the multi-camera comedy would revolve around a daughter's return to the fold of her large, eccentric, ethnic family after her husband takes a job in her hometown. It was directed by [[Pamela Fryman]] and starred [[Annie Potts]], [[Desi Lydic]], [[Jack Thompson (actor)|Jack Thompson]], [[Mather Zickel]], [[Sasha Alexander]], [[Tinsley Grimes]], and [[Todd Stashwick]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=Littleton|first1=Cynthia|title=Boomer back on track with Berman|url=https://variety.com/2008/scene/markets-festivals/boomer-back-on-track-with-berman-1117991784/|access-date=January 31, 2018|work=Variety|date=September 7, 2008}}</ref>

===''Malcolm in the Middle''===
Boomer based the [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox]] sitcom ''[[Malcolm in the Middle]]'' on his life story.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barney |first1=Chuck |title=Oddball 'Malcolm' puts some life back into TV comedy |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/green-bay-press-gazette-oddball-malcolm/205522037/ |access-date=September 7, 2026 |work=Green Bay Press-Gazette |date=January 27, 2000 |page=D-12}}</ref> [[Pilot (Malcolm in the Middle)|The pilot episode of the series]] premiered on January 9, 2000, and was watched by 22.5 million viewers, while the second episode, "Red Dress" (premiered on January 16, 2000), was watched by 26 million viewers. Boomer wrote two episodes, starting with the pilot episode and then "Francis Escapes" and directed five episodes: "Opera," "Stilts," "Reese vs. Stevie," "Bride of Ida," and the series finale, "Graduation." Boomer received a [[Primetime Emmy Award]] for [[Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Writing for a Comedy Series|Outstanding Writing for a Comedy Series]] for the pilot episode. The series completed its six-year run on May 14, 2006, after seven seasons and 151 episodes.

In April 2026, a four-episode continuing miniseries of the show, ''[[Malcolm in the Middle: Life's Still Unfair]]'', also created by Boomer, was released on [[Hulu]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Porter |first1=Rick |title='Malcolm in the Middle' Creator, Director Detail How 'Life's Still Unfair' Came Together |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-features/how-malcolm-in-the-middle-lifes-still-unfair-came-together-1236561226/ |access-date=September 7, 2026 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=April 10, 2026}}</ref>

